{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"larval-tribal-tigermoth","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Report\n\nTwo agents measured the store write lock held ~12 minutes by a process that had ALREADY EXITED.\n\nInvestigated under bug royal-weariless-zebrafish (10c3-5a33-2e25-4584). Two hypotheses REFUTED:\n\n* **Not an fd leak.** `os.open` fds are non-inheritable under PEP 446 (verified: `FD_CLOEXEC` set); there is no `os.fork()` anywhere in `src/rebar`; every lock-held spawn uses `close_fds=True`; `gc.autoDetach=false` / `maintenance.autoDetach=false` are set at `init.py:110-154`. A flock cannot outlive its process.\n* **Not `start=-` defeating liveness.** On macOS the stamp is `host=... ns=- pid=... start=-`; host matches, both `ns` sides are `None` and compare equal, and a genuinely DEAD pid IS reclaimed at `lock.py:553-554`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`lock.py:555-560`:\n\n```python\nif not _pid_alive(pid): return True\nstamped_start = None if fields[\"start\"] == _STAMP_UNKNOWN else fields[\"start\"]   # None on macOS\ncurrent_start = _process_start_time(pid)                                          # None on macOS\nif stamped_start is not None and current_start is not None and ...: return True   # DEAD CODE\nreturn False\n```\n\n`_process_start_time()` reads `/proc` and so ALWAYS returns `None` on macOS. The recycled-pid discriminator at `:557` is therefore **inert by construction**, and the function falls to `return False`. When the original holder is dead but its pid NUMBER has been recycled by an unrelated live process, `_pid_alive()` reports \"live\" and the lock is judged fresh.\n\nThat branch is deliberately **exempt from the 1h `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop** (`lock.py:516-519`, \"never on a timer alone\") because a live pid is treated as positive proof of liveness. On a platform with no start time it is not proof — it is a bare pid-number collision. Net effect: an **unbounded** wedge. Recycling is live on this host (`kern.maxproc: 16000`; the counter has already wrapped past the observed stamp pid).\n\nSecondary, bounded (1h) wedge: `_reclaim_mkdir_lock` removes the owner file (`lock.py:581`) then rmdirs (`lock.py:585`), both best-effort. If the rmdir fails, an owner-less dir remains that only the age ceiling can clear (`lock.py:528-531`).\n\n## Suggested direction\n\nApply `_mkdir_lock_age_exceeds_ceiling` on the `lock.py:560` branch **only when `current_start` is unavailable** — i.e. when the \"live pid\" verdict is unqualified and therefore not a real liveness proof. That preserves \"never break a live owner on a timer alone\" on Linux while bounding the no-/proc wedge.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A dead-holder lock whose pid has been recycled is reclaimed after a bounded interval on a platform with no `/proc`, verified by a test that stamps a lock with a live-but-unrelated pid and asserts reclamation past the ceiling\n- [ ] A genuinely live holder with a corroborating start time is still NEVER broken on a timer alone, verified by a Linux-shaped test asserting refusal\n- [ ] The owner-less-dir path is bounded, verified by a test","id":"ab00-1407-2da3-42fd","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Stale mkdir lock is UNBOUNDED on platforms without /proc: recycled pid defeats reclamation"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786531879752031001,"uuid":"d22ce294-995c-4f88-98e1-ade4712a1999"}